@prefix ag:   <http://example.org/agora#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<http://example.org/agora/mqtt> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Agora MQTT binding" ;
    rdfs:comment """One way of reaching things: a broker and named channels on it. Everything here is a VARIANT — a society could speak Modbus to its sensors, or read a GPIO pin with no bus at all, and would swap this module without touching perception, market or actuation. Note what is NOT here: any capability. Which protocol a device speaks changes nothing an agent must decide, so it earns a binding on the device, not a name in the agent's abilities.""" .

#################  The bus  #################
# In the WORLD, not in the environment: a topic means nothing without the broker it is on,
# and every agent must agree on the bus or they are not in the same society at all. The only
# thing a process is told by its environment is where the belief base is; the belief base
# then says everything else, including this.

ag:MessageBus a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Message bus" ;
    rdfs:comment "A broker agents reach each other through. Found by type; when there is more than one, resources name theirs with ag:onBus." .

ag:brokerHost a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:MessageBus ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "broker host" .
ag:brokerPort a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:MessageBus ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "broker port" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where a principal that authenticates by password connects. The boards use this: a device that deep-sleeps between readings would pay a TLS handshake on every wake." .
ag:brokerTlsPort a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:MessageBus ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "broker TLS port" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where a principal that authenticates by CERTIFICATE connects. Optional: a world without it has no mTLS door, and its agents use the password one. Same bus and the same ACL either way — this is how you prove who you are, not which society you are in." .
ag:onBus a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:range ag:MessageBus ;
    rdfs:label "on bus" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which bus this resource is reachable on. Optional while a society has one." .

#################  Channels  #################

ag:readingTopic a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "reading topic" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where a sensor PUBLISHES what it read. Several sensors may name the SAME topic: one board carrying two peripherals is one MQTT client with one credential, so it publishes one message and each sensor takes its own value out of it with ag:readingPointer." .

ag:readingPointer a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "reading pointer" ;
    rdfs:comment "WHICH value in the payload is this sensor's, as a JSON Pointer (RFC 6901, April 2013, Standards Track): `/temperature`, `/readings/0/rh`. Absent means `/value`, which is what every single-property device already publishes — so this is invisible until a device reports more than one thing. A POINTER and not a JSONPath (RFC 9535) because a pointer identifies exactly ONE value, which is exactly what a reading is; a query language returns a nodelist and would need a collapse rule invented on top of it. Nothing here says how the bytes became a document — that is the codec, and JSON is the only one. See knowledge/decisions/a-reading-is-one-value-so-it-is-pointed-at.md." .
ag:commandTopic a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "command topic" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where a device LISTENS for instructions: a cadence for a sensor, a dose for a valve. Only a device that accepts instruction has one." .
ag:statusTopic a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "status topic" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where a device REPORTS what it actually did. A valve publishes here after it has opened, so the difference between a command being sent and water having flowed is on the wire rather than assumed. `firmware/pump-valve` has always published one, deriving the name in C as its command topic plus \"/status\"; stating it here is the same correction agora-firmware made when it found the sensor firmware building its own topics from PLANT_ID." .
ag:eventTopic a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "event topic" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where an agent announces something it noticed. Peers may listen; nobody is obliged to." .

#  A device carrying these properties is reached over MQTT; that is what selects the driver
#  the perception module talks to it through. An agent may hold sensors on different
#  bindings at once and still have ONE attention policy, which is the point of keeping
#  protocol out of the capability.
